// **The marker bed** -- the bed you can find again, and until 2026-08-30 the
// only red in the ground.
//
// `Reports/worldgen-appearance-audit-2026-08-29.md` §5 measured four to nine
// colours covering half of everything the player sees below the skyline, and
// every one of them is a grey, a brown or a tan: the mineral world spans
// 92..192 in value and essentially one hue. Ironstone is the entry that is
// not on that list. It is deliberately **rare** -- roughly one bed in
// twenty-five -- because a marker bed's whole value is that you can find it
// again, and a rock you see everywhere marks nothing.
//
// **Its red is now a warm brown**, and that was the owner's call rather than
// a drift: shown the six rocks he asked twice for a shared palette and less
// contrast, so ironstone was pulled onto the same hue as everything else
// along with the rest (see the note above `colors`). It is still the warmest
// and one of the darker entries, and it is still rare, so it still marks --
// but it no longer shouts.
//
// Set relative to `stone.ron`; see
// `Reports/rock-vocabulary-design-2026-08-29.md` for the set.
(
    name: "ironstone",
    display: "Ironstone",
    kind: Solid,
    // The heaviest of the bedded rocks -- it is a third iron.
    density: 3.4,

    //   0  fresh    dark rust. Random picks stay here.
    //   1  weathered  **brighter, not darker.** Iron is the one rock here
    //      whose exposed skin gains saturation: it oxidises to a bloom. So
    //      an ironstone bed announces itself hardest exactly where the
    //      player can see it, on the open face, and is quieter in a cut.
    // **These numbers are derived, not eyeballed, and the derivation is the
    // only way to change them safely.** Shown the six-rock world the owner
    // said twice, unprompted, *"the color changes between layers is too much.
    // There is too much contrast. There should be more of a shared color
    // palette"*, and on the first correction *"I would drop the contrast even
    // more"*. So every colour here is the file's original pulled 86% onto one
    // shared warm-grey hue -- the mean of the six rocks' fresh families,
    // renormalised to unit brightness -- after the whole material has been
    // shifted half way toward the global mean brightness.
    //
    // **The shift is per material, never per colour.** The four tones inside
    // one rock are what make a bed legible, and `stone.ron`'s own comment
    // records that squeezing *those* made the banding vanish from the render
    // entirely. Shifting the material bodily leaves every internal spread at
    // exactly its original value and compresses only the gap between rocks,
    // which is what the complaint was about: measured, that gap went from 116
    // brightness points to 57 while no rock's own spread moved at all.
    //
    // Full account and the b50 measurements either side:
    // `Reports/worldgen-relief-2026-08-30.md` section 5.5.
    colors: [
        // 0: fresh
        (121, 101, 88),
        (108, 90, 79),
        (138, 115, 101),
        (96, 79, 70),
        // 1: weathered -- rust bloom
        (140, 115, 99),
        (125, 102, 88),
        (159, 133, 115),
        (111, 91, 78),
        // 2: **damp** -- rock below the water table, and rock a pond or a
        // stream is standing against.
        //
        // Derived rather than eyeballed, and stated so it can be re-derived:
        // family 0 multiplied by (0.78, 0.79, 0.83). Wet rock is darker
        // because the water film kills the diffuse scatter off the grain,
        // and it is *cooler* because the loss is smallest at the blue end --
        // so a single multiplier per channel says the whole thing, and no
        // rock in the set needs its own artistic judgement about being wet.
        // It is one byte, written at genesis, exactly like the other two.
        (100, 83, 74),
        (90, 74, 66),
        (113, 95, 84),
        (80, 67, 59),
    ],
    base_colors: 4,

    // Hard, and the reason a marker bed survives to be a marker: it stands
    // proud of the softer beds either side of it as a rib on the face.
    max_unsupported_span: 20,
    attached_span_bonus: 13,
    support_cost_below: 1,
    support_cost_beside: 1,
    support_cost_above: 3,

    blast_resistance: 1.5,

    // Blocky nodules: {8, 16, 32, 64}. Fewer rungs than limestone, all of
    // them large -- ironstone does not grade down into grit, it comes off in
    // lumps or not at all.
    fragment_floor: 3,
    fragment_rungs: 4,

    joint_spacing: 17.0,

    breaks_into: "rubble",
    heat_conductivity: 0.1,
    rock: true,
)
